     1	# An ontology from a stack of papers
     2	
     3	You have a folder of research papers, and a question that crosses them:
     4	**which methods descend from which?** A model that reads papers can
     5	answer quickly. The answers are fluent, and some of them are wrong. A
     6	paper may say a method was *evaluated*, not *built on*. It may quote
     7	someone else's claim, or deny it. Two fields may give two different
     8	methods the same name.
     9	
    10	GND 0.4.0 lets you build one checked ontology over many papers. The agent
    11	still does the reading. GND keeps each claim tied to its passage in the
    12	document version it was read from ([where a quote may lie](../reference/anchors.md#where-a-span-may-lie)), refuses
    13	to join names nobody decided, and proves or refuses every answer. This
    14	page follows one stack of sixteen papers from eleven fields through three
    15	acts.
    16	
    17	<div class="app-figure" markdown="1" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="The three acts of the papers story">
    18	
    19	![Sixteen papers from eleven fields flow through three acts. Act 1, who is who: keep who said each claim and the passage, review each statement, decide which names are the same method. Act 2, one vocabulary: read every paper the same way, merge spellings and check a catalogue, flag dates that run backwards. Act 3, depth and change: answer or say unknown, know what was not read, revise and give each claim a verdict.](../assets/applications/papers-journey.svg)
